   504                              <1> ; genstart -- start a burst at a known phase of the clock: the generator
   505                              <1> ; from reset, [phd] reads of SSIOCTR as a delay, then the transmitter and
   506                              <1> ; receiver.  The first word must already be in SSIOTBUF.
   507                              <1> ;
   508                              <1> ; Why.  The transmitter starts in step with the baud-rate generator, and
   509                              <1> ; how a burst begins depends on the generator's phase at that instant
   510                              <1> ; (Intel's figures 12-7 and 12-8).  Free running, that phase is set by
   511                              <1> ; the time since the last burst: about the same throughout a run, since
   512                              <1> ; the code repeats, but different from run to run.  v10 shows it: CMD0
   513                              <1> ; answered in some runs, not at all in others, and a different code path
   514                              <1> ; (the recovery) turned a good run bad.  Restarting the generator each
   515                              <1> ; burst makes the phase repeatable; v5 did that with no delay and got a
   516                              <1> ; bad phase every time.  So step 3 tries delays until CMD0 answers, and
   517                              <1> ; step 9 does the same at each speed.  All with interrupts off: the delay
   518                              <1> ; is counted in instructions and must not be stretched.
   519                              <1> NPHD		equ	8		; delays tried: 0 to 7 reads
