   538                              <1> ; spi_xfer -- move CX words (at least one) through the SSIO, full duplex.
   539                              <1> ;   SI	the bytes to send, two a word, first byte first; 0 sends FFh
   540                              <1> ;   DI	where to put the bytes received, the same way; 0 discards them
   541                              <1> ; Returns AX = the last word received, first byte in AL; CF set if the
   542                              <1> ; SSIO stopped answering.  Advances SI and DI when they are not 0.
   543                              <1> ; Clobbers BX, CX, DX.
   544                              <1> ;
   545                              <1> ; The transmitter is double buffered: the next word goes into the holding
   546                              <1> ; buffer while the last one shifts, and the receiver hands over each word
   547                              <1> ; as it completes.  If the buffer were not refilled in time the
   548                              <1> ; transmitter would send the old word again and the receiver would lose
   549                              <1> ; one, so the burst runs with interrupts held off.  The last word is
   550                              <1> ; followed by a disable, which lets it finish and then stops the clock.
   551                              <1> ;
   552                              <1> ; The baud-rate generator runs whether or not the transmitter does, and
   553                              <1> ; the transmitter starts in step with it, so how a burst begins depends
   554                              <1> ; on the generator's phase at that moment (Intel's figures 12-7, 12-8).
   555                              <1> ; One way the card counts an edge the slave receiver does not, and every
   556                              <1> ; byte comes in a bit early.  Free running, a command burst always gets
   557                              <1> ; through and its R1 locates the offset (v3, v4); but the offset can
   558                              <1> ; differ from one burst to the next above 385 kHz (v4), so a data command
   559                              <1> ; goes in one burst with its data (xact_burst).  Restarting the
   560                              <1> ; generator for every burst to fix the phase stopped the card hearing
   561                              <1> ; commands at all (v5), so it runs free.
