   851                              <1> ; mkframe -- command AL, argument EBX, CRC byte AH into the frame: FF,
   852                              <1> ; 40|cmd, the argument big end first, the CRC, FF.  The FFs before it (the
   853                              <1> ; preamble) and after it (the window), are never written.  Clears the
   854                              <1> ; trace and the SSIO error flags.
   855                              <1> ;
   856                              <1> ; Why a preamble.  The transmitter starts in step with the free-running
   857                              <1> ; baud-rate generator; caught high, the clock pin goes from floating low
   858                              <1> ; straight to high, an edge the card counts -- and DI at that instant is
   859                              <1> ; still floating, held LOW by R7.  So the card can see a 0 before the
   860                              <1> ; burst's first bit, takes it for a command's start bit, and swallows the
   861                              <1> ; next 48 bits as a command of its own: CMD0 went unanswered in v3's first
   862                              <1> ; run, v5, v7 and v8, card reinserted or not, and was answered in the
   863                              <1> ; others -- a matter of phase.  Twenty bytes of FF first let any such
   864                              <1> ; phantom command end, be answered (illegal command) and finish before
   865                              <1> ; the real frame; the search for R1 starts after the frame, past the
   866                              <1> ; phantom's answer.
